sequenceDiagram
  %% Target design: stage 6 of the command development pipeline (feature-flow section 7). When a release that changed playbooks, pins or lock files, a daily run or an operator starts it, every task VM host brings its current task image and topology seed up to the release, in parallel, and saves them as the new ones: no build from the recipe; ready slots built from the old image are replaced one at a time. Nothing is copied between hosts.
  %% status: draft
  %% command: development pipeline stage 6 · per-host task image and seed refresh
  %% kind: pipeline.6-task-image-seed-refresh
  autonumber
  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue
  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green
  %% lane H icon=server pill=each host tone=amber
  %% lane VM icon=server pill=target tone=gray
  %% lane SL icon=server pill=pool tone=purple
  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API
  participant SW as Swamp<br/>one run per host
  participant H as Task VM host<br/>Incus over ssh
  participant VM as Refresh VM<br/>a task VM
  participant SL as Ready slots<br/>stopped task VMs
  Note over GW: Start the refresh<br/>release · daily · operator
  %%> Triggers:
  %%> - Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review (2026-11-06), container inner topology: a release whose commit changed playbooks, pins or lock files starts the refresh of every host, and a daily Schedule starts it too; both only dispatch the admission below and replace the triggers #3037 removed with the nested-VM path
  %%> - a code-only release needs no refresh: a fleet runs the checkout it is given
  %%> - an operator runs `php artisan task-vms:refresh-image HOST` or `task-vms:refresh-images`
  %%> - a host with a refresh open returns it; a host that refuses (an image build runs, no capacity) is skipped
  %%> Built (task_vm.image_refresh, workflow `operation-task-vm-image-refresh`):
  %%> - the run of one host, admitted like `tasks:prep`: a group of its own in the proof Project, placed on that host only, with the whole intent captured in the admission of provision; only the clone step calls the Gateway, to mint its read credential
  %%> - a repeat admission for a host returns the open run
  %%> Hosts:
  %%> - Linux only: the task VM hosts beast and shark, with Incus
  %%> Requirements per host:
  %%> - an active task VM host with no refresh running and one free large slot for the refresh VM
  %%> - the Gateway has the release commit, GitHub App read access to the repository, and the proof Swamp Club key
  GW->>SW: Start one run per host<br/>all in parallel
  %%> Why:
  %%> - each host refreshes its own image and seed, and builds its own fleet when it has none: copying them between hosts is not a supported flow, because every supported flow has to be proven again and again (Nick, 2026-11-06)
  %%> - a second admission for a host returns the open task
  loop each task VM host, in parallel
    SW->>H: Start a refresh VM<br/>current image · seed
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - starts a task VM from the host's current task image, with a clone of its current topology seed, as for any task
    %%> Built: the child `operation-task-provision`, which does this step and the next; the VM starts from the image the host promoted last, whatever its recipe or age
    %%> Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review:
    %%> - the refresh VM keeps the root disk of the host's base VM (20 GiB), not the larger disk of a topology VM, because a published image is as large as its VM's root disk and no VM can be smaller than its image; the first smoke of a 40 GiB image failed on it
    %%> Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review:
    %%> - the refresh VM is always a large task VM (12 GiB, 6 CPUs, 3 slots), because it holds the nested fleet (containers limited to 10 GiB) and builds it when the seed has no ready copies; the Gateway sets the size at the admission of the refresh, so no Project size list decides it (a group cannot declare a topology, so the size is passed to the placement, not declared); a medium VM (8 GiB) lost its nested Gateway to the out-of-memory killer in two runs on beast
    %%> - a refresh therefore needs 3 free slots of its host and is refused with `tasks.capacity_unavailable` otherwise; the operator tries again
    SW->>VM: Start the fleet<br/>container copies · release
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - checks out the release commit in the refresh VM, with `composer install` and `bun install` where a lock file changed
    %%> - starts the nested fleet from the current ready copies with that checkout as their disk device, and runs the apply step (state binds, token re-mint, migrations, restarts), as in stage 2
    %%> Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review (2026-11-06), container inner topology:
    %%> - a release is boot, converge, save; nothing is built. Measured on shark (`ebe3e67` to `3046d25`): 3 min 6 s in all; start 4 s, apply about 30 s, `node:converge` 30-41 s per Node, save 17 s
    %%> Built: all of it is the child `task-nested-fleet` of `operation-task-provision` (the start with the checkout disk device, the apply, the readiness check with Doctor, and the `base` snapshot), so the refresh saves ready copies only from a fleet that passed its readiness check
    opt no usable ready copies
      SW->>VM: Build the fleet<br/>task_vm.fleet_build
      %%> Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review (Nick approved 2026-11-06):
      %%> - a refresh of a host whose seed holds no usable ready copies (a new host, a new image or copies of an older format) runs the nested fleet build as a child in the refresh VM (chart `docs/cli/tasks/vm-fleet-build.mmd`), automatically and with no flag; the save of the ready copies follows
      %%> - the step is skipped when the provision started the fleet from ready copies; the run needs no check of its own, because a failed build fails the run before the save
      %%> - the refresh admits the fleet-build intent with the rest of its intent, before its VM exists: the host keys of the guest are pinned by the VM step, as for the ready copies, and the build takes the proof Club key as the save does
      %%> - the waits of the build stop at once when the nested Gateway guest is not running (an Ansible task with `until` and `retries`, chart `docs/cli/tasks/vm-fleet-build.mmd`), and then name every nested container that is not running; a dead app VM ends the Operation it enrolls
      %%> Built: the step `Build the fleet`, the child `operation-task-vm-fleet-build`
    end
    opt playbooks or pins changed since the current image
      SW->>VM: Converge once<br/>outer VM · nested Nodes
      %%> What it does:
      %%> - image playbooks on the outer VM; role playbooks and pins on the nested Nodes, through the nested Orbit's `node:converge`
      %%> - the same step as in the proof CI (stage 4)
    %%> Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review:
    %%> - the run always converges: a VM at the pins reports no change in about a minute, so the Gateway needs no check of what changed; the `opt` stays as the chart of the intent
    %%> Built: the outer VM, as the converge of the provision (`Converge once` of stage 2), which this run reuses instead of a step of its own; the nested Nodes as the step `Converge the nested Nodes`, the child `task-nested-converge`: on a fleet that started from ready copies, the nested Gateway admits one `node.converge` per Node it manages (`orbit:node-converge`, every Node but the operator) from the checkout, and the step waits for each; a fleet the refresh built is already at the release and skips it
    end
    SW->>VM: Save the ready copies<br/>key-free · boot-checked
    %%> Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review (2026-11-06):
    %%> - the refresh has no token step: every provision that starts a fleet renews the Swamp tokens of the nested Gateway (stage 2), so the age of the copies does not matter
    %%> - the copies carry format 4 (containers): the control plan the renewal reads is on the nested Gateway; copies of an older format are not used
    %%> Built: the child `operation-task-vm-ready-copies`, the kind `task_vm.ready_copies`, on the refresh VM
    SW->>VM: Stop the refresh VM<br/>its seed disk is complete
    %%> Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review (Nick approved 2026-11-06):
    %%> - the refresh VM yields only its seed disk and is never published; it is stopped so that the copy of the disk is consistent
    %%> - the seed disk holds only the `node-template` and the `fleet-ready-*` copies; the running nested guests are on the VM's own disk and go with the VM
    %%> - this removes what existed because an enrolled VM was published: the withdraw step, the identity check and the `enrolled` branch of `task-vm-image-clean`
    SW->>H: Build the image<br/>clean builder · phases
    %%> Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review (Nick approved 2026-11-06):
    %%> - the image is published from a builder: a clone of the image the refresh VM started from (its fingerprint, `candidate.source`), named `candidate.builder`, never enrolled, with no seed attached
    %%> - the builder is brought to the pins of the release by the phases `software`, `tools` and `check`, then cleaned by `task-vm-image-clean` (cloud-init, machine-id, SSH host keys, trim, stop), which has no enrolled branch
    %%> - the sequence from the launch to the publication is one child workflow, `task-vm-image-builder`, which the refresh runs now; the image build (`task_vm.image_build`) keeps its own jobs until a follow-up moves it onto the child, because its result reader names those steps
    %%> - the assert that the builder has no `topology-seed` pool comes with the removal of the surgery below: the builder is cloned from an image that may still hold the stale records, so the assert cannot pass before the lineage is clean
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - clones the builder, waits for its guest agent and cloud-init, runs the phases, cleans and stops it, publishes it under a candidate alias and reads its fingerprint
    %%> Risks the run accepts:
    %%> - the ready copies are made on the refresh VM from the old image, and the image comes from the builder; the next provision is the check of the pair, and `-previous` (image and old seed) is the rollback
    %%> - the `incus admin sql` surgery in the seed attach stays until every host serves an image whose lineage never held the seed pool, for example one rebuilt from stock with `task_vm.image_build`; a later PR deletes it
    SW->>H: Enroll, converge check VM<br/>on the boot-check VM
    %%> Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review:
    %%> - a release can trigger a refresh with nobody watching, and a refresh starts from the image the host serves, so one bad image would reach every later provision; the candidate is therefore enrolled and converged before it is promoted
    %%> - on the throwaway VM of the boot check, after its check phase, the run enrolls the guest with the same bootstrap steps as an enrollment (account, key, sudoers file, observer install and verify), then converges it to the pins with the phases `software`, `tools` and `check`
    %%> - the bootstrap steps are one child workflow, `task-vm-bootstrap`, which `task-vm-enrollment` calls too, so the check and a real enrollment cannot drift apart
    %%> - the promotion waits for both steps; a candidate that cannot be enrolled or converged promotes nothing
    %%> Built: the steps `Enroll the check VM` (the child `task-vm-bootstrap`, which `task-vm-enrollment` calls too) and `Converge the check VM` (the child `task-vm-image-ansible`, phases `software`, `tools` and `check`), before the seed copy; the enrollment of the check VM is the guard against enrollment files left in an image
    %%> Not covered:
    %%> - the WireGuard peer, the observer start and the nested fleet: they need a Gateway-admitted Node, so the next provision is their check
    SW->>H: Boot-check, promote<br/>under the host lock
    %%> Built: the smoke is the child `task-vm-image-smoke` and the alias move is the child `task-vm-image-promote`, the same children as the image build
    %%> Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review:
    %%> - the new image has a new fingerprint, and a seed is named from its image, so the new seed is a copy of the refresh VM's seed disk under the new name (`zfs promote` after the copy, so refreshes form no clone chain); the old image and the old seed stay under their own names, and the old image keeps the alias `-previous`, which is the rollback copy
    %%> - the Gateway records the new image as the host's latest promoted build when the run ends, then cancels the group of the run, which destroys the refresh VM
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - boots a throwaway copy of each ready copy and of the image to check them, then promotes both and destroys the refresh VM
    %%> - keeps the old image and seed as `-previous`, the rollback copy; cleanup always runs
    %%> - a failed step keeps the old image and seed: the builder, and with it the publication and the promotion, run only after the VM step and the save of the ready copies passed, so a VM whose fleet failed its readiness check promotes nothing
    SW->>SL: Replace old ready slots<br/>one at a time
    %%> Target, not built:
    %%> - destroys a ready slot made from the old image, enrols a new one from the new image, then takes the next
    %%> - a slot is always ready; a slot in use keeps running on its own clone
    %%> - when the image did not change, nothing is replaced
    opt a new host or a new base OS
      SW->>H: Build from the recipe<br/>once, not per release
      %%> What it does:
      %%> - builds the task image from the stock image with the recipe phases (`task_vm.image_build`) and the first seed (`task_vm.seed_refresh`)
      %%> - every later release only brings these up to date
    end
  end
  alt every host refreshed
    SW-->>GW: Host refreshed<br/>terminal event per host
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - each host reports its own result; one slow host does not hold the others
    Note over GW: Record the new seed<br/>claims use it
    %%> Result:
    %%> - the next task VM and the next proof start from the new seed, which holds the running release
    %%> - no fleet test follows; the seed is checked only by its own throwaway boot
  else a build or refresh failed
    SW--xGW: Host not refreshed<br/>previous seed stays
    %%> On failure:
    %%> - the failed step is named; the host keeps the image and seed it had, and the next trigger tries again
    Note over GW: Alert · try again<br/>next trigger
    %%> Result:
    %%> - the host keeps the image and seed it had; the other hosts are not affected
  end
